= Cornell Notes: Causes of the French Revolution

*Course:* European History #h(1fr) *Date:* #h(8em) *Unit:* Age of Revolution

#v(0.5em)

#table(
  columns: (1fr, 2.6fr),
  inset: 9pt,
  stroke: 0.6pt + luma(120),
  fill: (col, row) => if row == 0 { luma(230) } else if col == 0 { luma(246) } else { none },
  [*Cue / Questions*], [*Notes*],

  [What was the state's financial problem?],
  [
    - Debt from the Seven Years' War and support for the American Revolution
    - Royal expenses high; tax system unfair and inefficient
    - Calonne and Necker both tried reforms; the Assembly of Notables (1787) refused new taxes on privileged groups
    - Crown close to bankruptcy by 1788
  ],

  [How was society organized?],
  [
    - *First Estate:* clergy (about 0.5% of population), largely tax-exempt, collected tithe
    - *Second Estate:* nobility (about 1-2%), tax-exempt, held most high office and feudal dues
    - *Third Estate:* everyone else (about 97%), paid taille, gabelle, and most other taxes
    - Wealthy bourgeoisie resented exclusion from privilege; peasants bore feudal burdens
  ],

  [Which Enlightenment ideas mattered?],
  [
    - Montesquieu: separation of powers, limits on despotism
    - Voltaire: criticism of church influence and arbitrary justice
    - Rousseau: popular sovereignty, general will (_The Social Contract_, 1762)
    - Locke: natural rights to life, liberty, and property
    - Ideas spread through salons, cafés, pamphlets, and books
  ],

  [Why did leadership fail?],
  [
    - Louis XVI was indecisive and often gave in to pressure from nobles
    - Ministers were dismissed frequently, so no consistent reform policy
    - Political deadlock grew between the crown, parlements, and privileged orders
  ],

  [How did harvests and prices matter?],
  [
    - Poor harvests in 1788, followed by a severe hailstorm in July 1789
    - Bread made up a large share of wage earners' spending
    - Rising prices led to hunger, unrest, and food riots in towns and countryside
  ],

  [What triggered the crisis?],
  [
    - Crown called the Estates-General (May 1789) for the first time since 1614
    - Dispute over voting by order or by head; Third Estate formed the National Assembly (June 17)
    - Tennis Court Oath (June 20); royal troops gathered near Paris
    - Storming of the Bastille (July 14, 1789)
  ],
)

#v(1em)

== Summary

#rect(width: 100%, inset: 10pt, stroke: 0.6pt + luma(120))[
  The French Revolution grew from a fiscal crisis that the crown could not solve, because the tax system exempted the clergy and nobility. The Third Estate carried most of the burden and was inspired by Enlightenment ideas about rights and representation. Weak leadership, deadlock among elites, and food shortages turned the crisis into open revolt once the Estates-General met in 1789.
]

#v(1em)

